    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                We are going to continue regression analysis for other penguins
                in the <q>palmer_penguins.csv</q> file.
                You can take and adapt the code from class as you see fit.
                Make sure to import the <q>palmer_penguins.csv</q> file.
                Isolate the Gentoo species and create a new data frame with this
                data.
                This is the data we will be working with for this problem - we
                will only consider the Gentoo penguins.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What islands have Gentoo penguins?
                    Do we need a color_dictionary anymore?
                    Why or why not?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Use Python to find the linear regression that inputs the
                    bill length and outputs the body mass.
                    Plot the bill length on the x-axis and the body mass on the
                    y-axis, along with the predicted model.
                    Print the <m>R^2</m> value for this linear regression.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Do the same linear regression, but this time input the bill
                    depth, and output the body mass.
                    Plot the model and the data below.
                    Print the <m>R^2</m> value for this linear regression.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Do the same linear regression, but this time input the
                    flipper length, and output the body mass.
                    Plot the model and the data below.
                    Print the <m>R^2</m> value for this linear regression.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Which input was a better predictor for the body mass?
                    Explain.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Adapt the linear regression to a multi-linear regression,
                    inputting the features bill length, bill depth, and flipper
                    length, and outputting the body mass.
                    Print the coefficients ( <c>.coef_</c> and
                    <c>.intercept_</c> ).
                    Which feature/input is the most important predictor of body
                    mass?
                    Does this match Part (e)?
                    Explain your reasoning.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Use the multi-linear regression model found in Part (f) to
                    predict the body mass of a penguin with a bill length of
                    57.5 mm, a bill depth of 16.5 mm, and a flipper length of
                    226 mm.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </exercise>
</worksheet>